-# CritAssim — round 48 assimilation attack
-
-## Axes
-Depth: knowledge-ground novelty and rule coverage. Coverage: staged rows/entry count is not equivalent to new surface. Calibration: evidence confidence; no claim of complete subject dedup from filenames.
-
-## Finding
-**Refusal of the 77-entry/132-row package as a whole is defensible; refusal of every package contribution as duplicate/padding is not supported.** The package is plainly not new ground as a blanket count: the round-history list traverses rounds 1–16 with large clusters on adb, REA, Japanese/Chinese resources, unblob/Sasquatch, AVM and binary lifters. The merge report itself flags a duplicate target and requires folding, not blind append. But the lane already assimilated meaningful portions and also has a potentially novel, bounded early tool-contract slice.
-
-Evidence at package interface `/home/vgpnk/Projects/shokuknow/merge/kaidoku/MERGE-REPORT.md`: lines 5–9 state 132 proposed rows, 77 proposed entries, and stale-at-packaging 105-row baseline; lines 19 onward enumerate subjects; lines 118–127 require checks and explicit duplicate folding. A live filename-normalized comparison found 138 lane markdown files, 77 package files, and zero identical normalized stems. That zero is *not* evidence of zero subject overlap: lane filenames are round/date-qualified while package filenames are topical. Do not use filename-set difference as a novelty result.
-
-### Rule-level duplicate evidence (strong)
-The lane already holds `corpus/knowledge/2026-10-26-unblob-sasquatch-dependency.md` and `2026-10-26-unblob-format-matrix.md`; the package separately proposes `unblob-sasquatch-dependency.md` and `unblob-format-matrix.md` (package report lines 19, 29). Prior lane review R41Assim (`.ufo/waves/round-41/w1/R41Assim.md:31-40`) determined the dependency entry re-derives existing ground and is a superset, including stronger pins; it explicitly recommended rejection/folding. Package lines 125–126 likewise direct duplicate folding. These should not become two rows/entries merely due to title/date differences.
-
-Other evident subject clusters need rule-level comparison before admission, not global rejection: adb host/device verb splits occur across package entries at rounds 2, 4–6; package round 6's “complete” alignment and round 2/4/5 narrower variants are likely successive claims, not automatically independent coverage. The lane already has `2026-10-13-r4u-06-adb-surface.md`; package round 4 `adb-verb-classification-join.md` is specifically about 2/22 split and upstream dispatch, so may add an upstream rule despite overlapping the measured subject. Likewise the package's `binwalk v3` `firmware-region-vocabulary.md` has already landed in lane as `2026-10-26-binwalk-v3-unit-of-work.md`; it is not an adoption candidate now. This is confirmed by `R41Assim.md:42-53`, which treated it as novel at that earlier base, specified the repaired row and entry name, and the current lane inventory contains the resulting file.
-
-### Candidate subset (tentative, not blanket approval)
-The most plausible genuinely new ground is package round 1's paired managed-assembly producer contract (`managed-assembly-division-of-labour.md`, dnfile metadata/offset versus dncil body), and Android package inspection split (`android-package-inspection-contract.md`, jadx output selection versus Apktool decode/rebuild/smali). These have precise producer-rule scope distinct from the broad count argument. Package round 1's crash-size ceiling (`recorded-crash-size-ceiling.md`) is also a plausible new rule, but likely overlaps existing crash-probe/inspection ground and needs direct rule comparison. These are candidates only: I did not open their full text or verify sources, anchors, or novelty against all 138 lane files; no admission verdict is established.
-
-## Smallest safe recommendation
-Reject bulk assimilation and positional S-number reuse. At minimum fold/reject the known duplicate unblob dependency and do not re-admit binwalk v3. For any candidate subset, compare each normative rule/claim and evidence anchor against the lane corpus (not just titles), re-open sources, then append only independently useful rules with per-document IDs and accepted row schema. Count distinct rules and resolved gaps, not package rows/files. No new corpus ground should be smuggled into round 48: the frozen round-48 claim file explicitly excludes domain surface and coverage expansion (`plans/round-48-claims.md:17-22`). Thus this is an attack on the *assimilation rationale*, not a recommendation to land the package in this round.
-
-## Evidence limits
-Read-only. No package-wide semantic matching, staged row-by-row source audit, census, or live source reopen was performed; those are **not run**. No command receipts for the exploratory Python comparison were saved, so its counts are informal observations, not citable quantitative proof. No installs. No lane production files modified.
